  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A permutation of length NNN is an array of length NNN that contains every integer from 111 to NNN exactly once.\n\nGiven NNN and KKK, find a permutation PPP of length NNN satisfying the following constraints:\n\n\nPi≠iP_i \\ne iPi​=i for all 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N\nPi mod K=i mod KP_i \\bmod K = i \\bmod KPi​modK=imodK for all 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N\nIn case no such permutation exists, output −1-1−1 instead.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains 222 integers - NNN and KKK.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output the following:\n\n\n\nIf a valid permutation exists, output NNN space-separated integers - P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​, representing the permutation you found.\nOtherwise, output −1-1−1.\n\nIf there are multiple valid permutations, you may print any of them.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\le T \\le 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤K≤N≤1001 \\le K \\le N \\le 1001≤K≤N≤100"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n4 2\n3 1\n4 3\n1 1",
        "output": "3 4 1 2\n2 3 1\n-1\n-1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: All the constraints are satisfied with P=[3,4,1,2]P = [3, 4, 1, 2]P=[3,4,1,2]. For example, we can check P1≠1P_1 \\ne 1P1​=1 but P1 mod 2=1 mod 2=1P_1 \\bmod 2 = 1 \\bmod 2 = 1P1​mod2=1mod2=1.\nTest case 222: Since K=1K = 1K=1, Pi mod K=i mod KP_i \\bmod K = i \\bmod KPi​modK=imodK holds by default. We only need Pi≠iP_i \\ne iPi​=i, so any permutation satisfying that works. Another possible answer is P=[3,1,2]P = [3, 1, 2]P=[3,1,2].\nTest case 333: No valid permutation exists."
      }
    ]
  },
